---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import Example from '../../components/Example.astro';
import CodeViewer from '../../components/CodeViewer.astro';
const base=import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const sections=[{id:'inheritance',title:'Inheritance'},{id:'exceptions',title:'Local exceptions'},{id:'custom-radius',title:'Custom corners'},{id:'custom-density',title:'Custom spacing'},{id:'custom-width',title:'Page width'},{id:'classes',title:'Classes and attributes'},{id:'custom-css',title:'Custom CSS'}];
---
<DocsLayout title="Styling and inheritance" active="styling" sections={sections}>
<p class="doc-kicker">LEARN</p><h1>Styling and inheritance</h1>
<p class="doc-lead">Configure appearance on html or a parent section. Native elements and component classes consume those shared settings wherever they apply.</p>
<section id="inheritance"><h2>What inherits?</h2><p>Theme, accent, shape, density and other shared attributes supply tokens or scoped styling. They affect documented targets, not every HTML element indiscriminately. Width affects containers; ratio affects media; variant affects actions, badges and alerts.</p>
<p>The nearest valid explicit value wins for its setting. Most unsupported values leave the inherited setting in effect. Material and independent button-material boundaries are compatibility exceptions: an unsupported value can stop a parent scope without selecting another finish. Never use invalid values as resets. Omitted settings use the shipped defaults. Values are not copied into child HTML attributes.</p></section>
<Example id="exceptions" title="Override only what differs" description="Both cards inherit the theme and shape. The warning alert changes only its semantic colour." html={'<section class="stack" data-theme="dark" data-shape="rounded"\n         data-accent="teal" data-variant="success">\n  <article class="card">\n    <h3>Shared settings</h3>\n    <div class="alert">Success: Your profile is complete.</div>\n  </article>\n  <article class="card">\n    <div class="alert" data-variant="warning">Warning: Verify your email.</div>\n  </article>\n</section>'}/>
<p><a href={base+'themes/palettes/'}>Palette examples and override rules</a>: configure data-palette on html or a parent. A nearer palette installs its complete colour set. On one element, explicit data-accent and data-tone override its primary and surface colours.</p>
<section id="font-selection"><h2>Choose the font</h2><p>Choose Customize → Typography and controls → Font. Default (Manrope) preserves the framework font; System uses the device UI font. Set <code>data-font="system"</code> on html or a section, and use <code>data-font="manrope"</code> for a local reset. Text, headings and native controls inherit it, while code remains monospace. The optional <code>reva-fonts.css</code> and adjacent font files supply Manrope; if the family is unavailable, the system fallback is used. An explicit <code>--re-font-heading</code> remains available for separate headings. Choices persist between documentation and demos and are included in downloaded HTML. The core remains CSS-only.</p></section>
<section id="custom-radius"><h2>Choose your own corners</h2><p>Select Customize → Surfaces → Shape → Custom. Link surface, field and action radiuses or edit them independently. Use sliders or numeric values in rem; zero creates square corners. Choices persist across documentation and demo pages. CSS exports and starting HTML work without the editor scripts.</p>
<CodeViewer id="custom-radius-tokens" lang="css" code={'/* Load after RevaCSS and use data-shape="custom". */\n@layer re.tokens {\n  :where([data-shape="custom"], :scope[data-shape="custom"]) {\n    --re-custom-surface-radius: 1rem;\n    --re-custom-field-radius: .4rem;\n    --re-custom-action-radius: 3rem;\n  }\n}'}/>
<p>Each group defaults to .75rem. Surface corners use <code>--re-radius</code>; styled text fields use <code>--re-field-radius</code>, falling back to the surface radius; buttons, badges and existing action-shaped components use <code>--re-button-radius</code>. These controls do not replace native checkbox/radio geometry. Local Square, Subtle, Rounded or Pill boundaries reset the three groups. Frame and Flush navbar rules retain their intentional square corners.</p></section>
<section id="custom-density"><h2>Choose your own spacing</h2><p>Select Customize → Layout → Density → Custom. Link the values or edit surface padding, control padding and content spacing independently with sliders or numeric inputs. Surface padding and content spacing use rem; control padding is a multiplier of each control’s normal comfortable padding. Cards multiply base surface padding by 1.5. Minimum control heights and safe-area padding remain in place.</p>
<CodeViewer id="custom-density-tokens" lang="css" code={'/* Load after RevaCSS and use data-density="custom". */\n@layer re.tokens {\n  :where([data-density="custom"], :scope[data-density="custom"]) {\n    --re-custom-surface-padding: 1.2rem;\n    --re-custom-control-density: .8;\n    --re-custom-content-space: .6rem;\n  }\n}'}/>
<p>Each group defaults to 1. Surface padding affects cards, preformatted code, fieldsets, disclosures, dialogs, drawers, sidebars, shell headers and empty states. Content spacing controls the existing shared margins and gaps; individual components retain their own proportions. Local Compact, Comfortable and Spacious presets reset all groups. The separate Gap setting scales layout gaps, and explicit component padding or gap overrides still take precedence. Editor choices persist across pages; exported CSS and starting HTML work without JavaScript.</p></section>
<section id="custom-width"><h2>Full and custom page widths</h2><p>Use <code>data-width="full"</code> for containers that fill their parent and retain 1rem side padding. Choose Customize → Layout → Page width → Custom to set a maximum width and side padding in rem. Maximum width includes padding; narrow containers reduce excessive side padding to keep content space. Narrow, Standard and Wide retain their existing 48rem, 75rem and 90rem caps and outer gutters.</p>
<CodeViewer id="custom-width-tokens" lang="css" code={'/* Load after RevaCSS and use data-width="custom". */\n@layer re.tokens {\n  :where([data-width="custom"], :scope[data-width="custom"]) {\n    --re-custom-max-width: 80rem;\n    --re-custom-side-padding: 2rem;\n  }\n}'}/>
<p>Use a <code>.container</code> inside the configured parent. Local width presets reset maximum width and padding. Navbar width remains controlled by <code>data-navbar-width</code>; a full navbar surface stays full while its content follows the page width. Reading-width limits such as <code>.prose</code> remain independent. Choices persist across pages and are included in CSS exports and downloaded starting HTML.</p></section>
<p>Current source adds local <code>data-layout="container|grid|row|stack"</code>, <code>data-surface="card"</code> and <code>data-reva</code> scoped boundaries. These are unavailable in published 2.0.0; existing classes remain supported. Native structural elements remain neutral. <a href={base+'guide/architecture/'}>Architecture, complete markup audit and inheritance matrix</a>.</p>
<section id="classes"><h2>Classes identify patterns</h2><p><a href={base+'reference/classes/'}>Browse the complete Classes API</a> for purposes, markup rules and stylesheet dependencies.</p><p>A native button already identifies its element. A generic div needs <code>class="alert"</code> to identify an alert pattern. Shared attributes then configure supported descendants.</p>
<p>Buttons, action inputs, .button links, badges and alerts share <code>data-variant</code>. Existing <code>secondary</code>, <code>danger</code> and <code>warning</code> button classes remain local overrides of parent variants. A valid variant directly on the action takes precedence over those classes. <code>outline</code> and <code>ghost</code> remain presentation classes. <code>data-appearance</code> still configures only badges and alerts. There are no rv- utility shortcuts yet; the rv- SVG symbol prefix is separate.</p>
<p>Shared size is consumed once for controls inside sized form labels and for spinners or badges inside actions. Explicit child size settings remain local overrides. Circular spinners retain their indicator shape; avatar aspect ratio stays square independently of the chosen boundary shape.</p>
<p><a href={base+'reference/components/'}>See the exact hook and supported options for each component.</a></p></section>
<section id="custom-css"><h2>Override with ordinary CSS</h2><p>The framework uses ordered CSS layers: re.tokens, re.base, re.components, re.buttons and re.utilities. Application styles outside a layer can override normal layered declarations. Load your custom stylesheet after RevaCSS for a clear project convention.</p>
<CodeViewer id="css-overrides" lang="css" label="Page and component customisation" code={':root {\n  --re-primary: #7041cf;\n  --re-primary-end: #5228a4;\n  --re-on-primary: #fff;\n}\n.project-card {\n  max-inline-size: 40rem;\n}'}/>
<p>Define both accent stops and a readable foreground when replacing colours. Scoped builds need custom properties on the .reva boundary or inside it because the boundary installs its own defaults. See <a href={base+'reference/tokens/'}>CSS token reference</a>.</p></section>
<p>For your own component, follow <a href={base+'guide/custom-components/'}>the extension contract and downloadable starter</a>. Reuse an existing surface hook where appropriate; colour-token inheritance alone does not apply material styling to arbitrary classes.</p>
<p>The documentation's optional Customize control shares one saved appearance profile with the demo pages. Demo navigation includes the same control and a Docs link; there is no extra documentation navbar above it. Choices apply immediately, follow you between pages and are included in HTML downloads. Reset clears the shared choices. These site controls are separate from the CSS-only framework.</p>
<p class="doc-note">Theme is a colour mode. Palette is a coordinated colour family; Default uses Blue Teal, and Mono is optional. Material is a surface treatment. <code>data-fill="solid"</code> removes gradients; <code>data-material="solid"</code> stops inherited Glass or Veil. These settings serve different purposes. <a href={base+'themes/veil/'}>Veil is deprecated; its stylesheet remains available for existing interfaces.</a></p>
</DocsLayout>
